const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How long does shipping from China to Spain take?',
    a: 'WorldFreightHub Europe route data records roughly 32 days by sea from Shanghai, Ningbo-Zhoushan or Shenzhen to Valencia on the current Cape-of-Good-Hope routing, within a 25–45 day planning range. The Red Sea reroute adds about +10–14 days versus the pre-crisis Suez routing (MEDIUM confidence, because these are planning estimates, not carrier promises). Rail into Europe runs roughly 18–22 days, but the final Iberia connection adds time. No verified Spain air transit is published in the snapshot — request that from your carrier.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How much does shipping from China to Spain cost?',
    a: 'The Valencia corridor has published ranges in the WorldFreightHub Europe route data: 20ft FCL $1,300–$5,000, 40ft FCL $1,800–$7,000, and LCL $70–$170 per CBM (all MEDIUM confidence). Rail is noted as roughly two to three times sea per container but has no published dollar figure, and air, express and DDP rates were not published in the snapshot — request those as itemised quotes. Rates swing sharply with Red Sea routing, fuel and season, so confirm a live figure before booking.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I ship to Valencia or Rotterdam for Spain?',
    a: 'For Spain or Portugal cargo, Valencia is usually the correct call. A Rotterdam discharge means a long, expensive haul across France before the container even enters Iberia, while a Valencia call puts it on the peninsula. China cargo often reaches Valencia via a Med transshipment rather than direct, and that extra move still usually beats northern-range trucking. Rotterdam is the better option only when the same container is consolidating multiple Northwest European destinations.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Valencia a direct-call port for China cargo?',
    a: 'Direct China calls are rare. Valencia’s route profile is the Mediterranean pattern: cargo transships at a hub such as Tanger Med or Malta, then feeders south into Valencia. That looks like a schedule penalty on paper, but for Iberian cargo it is often the cheapest total landed cost — do not let the word “direct” mislead you into pricing the wrong routing.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need an EORI before the goods arrive in Spain?',
    a: 'Yes. The EORI (Economic Operators Registration and Identification) number is required for any import into the EU and must be registered before your goods arrive. It is the single most common first-import stumbling block for China-to-Spain cargo, so start the registration before the vessel or train departs, not while it is discharging.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the VAT rate in Spain?',
    a: 'Spain applies a 21% standard VAT rate, with reduced rates of 10% and 4% on some goods. Import VAT is charged on the duty-inclusive value, so the effective tax is slightly above the headline 21% — budget the compound (CIF + duty) × 1.21 rather than a flat 21% on CIF.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between FCL and LCL for China to Spain?',
    a: 'FCL gives you exclusive use of a 20GP, 40GP or 40HQ and is usually the fit for large or heavy loads, priced per container. LCL consolidates your cargo with other importers and is priced per cubic metre. Illustratively, at a midpoint 20ft rate of $3,150 and LCL of $120/CBM, the break-even is about 26 CBM; below that LCL is usually cheaper, above it FCL starts to win. Request quotes on both sides of the split before deciding.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the €150 de-minimis threshold and how does IOSS work?',
    a: 'The EU abolished the €22 duty-free threshold in 2021, so import VAT now applies from the first euro on most consignments. The €150 figure is the VAT-related threshold, and the IOSS (Import One-Stop Shop) scheme simplifies VAT collection on sub-€150 consignments sold to EU buyers. Confirm the current thresholds and whether IOSS applies to your channel with Spanish Customs before relying on these figures.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What documents are needed for Spanish customs clearance?',
    a: 'The standard set is a commercial invoice, bill of lading (or rail consignment note for rail cargo), packing list and the correct 10-digit TARIC classification, backed by your EORI number. Regulated products also need CE marking evidence, and chemical substances need REACH registration — confirm the exact document set for your product class with a licensed Spanish broker.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need SABER or SASO to import into Spain?',
    a: 'No. SABER and SASO are Saudi-only conformity systems and do not apply to Spain or the EU. Do not copy a Saudi SABER/SASO step into a Spanish quote. Spain uses CE marking for regulated products and REACH for chemical substances instead, and any product-specific approval should be confirmed with Spanish Customs or your broker.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What are CE marking and REACH, and do they apply?',
    a: 'CE marking is the EU conformity mark required for a wide range of regulated products — electronics, toys, machinery and medical devices, among others — declaring the product meets EU requirements. REACH is the EU chemical regulation requiring registration for chemical substances. Whether either applies depends on your product class, so confirm the applicability and the required technical file before shipment.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do demurrage and detention differ in Spain?',
    a: 'Demurrage is the charge the terminal applies when import cargo stays in the port beyond the allowed free time after discharge. Detention is the charge the ocean carrier (or rail operator) applies when the container is kept beyond the equipment free time after collection. They are two separate clocks with two separate payees. Spanish free-time periods and per-day rates are not published in the verified snapshot — confirm both allowances in writing before booking and file the declaration promptly so neither clock runs.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does the Cape of Good Hope reroute affect my Spain shipment?',
    a: 'Since 2024 most China–Europe sailings reroute around the Cape of Good Hope to avoid the Red Sea, adding roughly +10–14 days versus the pre-crisis Suez routing. That is already built into the roughly 32-day Valencia planning figure, but it means your buffer should be wider than pre-crisis, and you should confirm the carrier’s actual routing before promising a delivery date.',
  },
];

