  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Why Spain needs its own China routing lens</h2>
    <p>
      Spain is the southern gate to the EU, and the country data shows <strong>Madrid</strong> as the
      capital, the currency as <strong>EUR</strong>, and a <strong>21% standard VAT</strong> layered on top
      of the <strong>EU Common Customs Tariff</strong> — a duty line that is uniform across every member
      state and varies only by HS code, typically <strong>0–12% for consumer goods</strong> with no
      China–EU free-trade agreement in force.
    </p>
    <p>
      The gate is the <strong>EORI number</strong>. It is mandatory for any import into the EU and must
      exist <em>before</em> the goods arrive — not after. That single administrative step stops more
      first-time importers than any tariff line, and it is why this page treats EORI as a compliance
      prerequisite rather than an afterthought.
    </p>
    <p>
      The differentiator is the <strong>Valencia-versus-Rotterdam decision</strong>. For cargo destined to
      Spain or Portugal, a Rotterdam discharge means a long, expensive haul across France, while a Valencia
      call puts the container on the Iberian doorstep. China cargo usually reaches Valencia via a
      Mediterranean transshipment at <strong>Tanger Med or Malta</strong> rather than direct — an extra
      move that looks like a penalty but usually costs less than trucking from the north. If your market is
      Iberia, price Valencia first.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. Indicative freight rates from China to Spain</h2>
      <p>
        Valencia has published benchmark depth in the Europe dataset: the WorldFreightHub Europe route
        data carries <strong>$1,300–$5,000 for a 20ft</strong>, <strong>$1,800–$7,000 for a 40ft</strong>
        and <strong>$70–$170 per CBM for LCL</strong>, all MEDIUM confidence. Rail is described in relative
        terms — roughly two to three times sea per container — but has no published dollar figure, and air,
        express and DDP were not published in the snapshot, so those stay LOW with a request-for-quote
        marker.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'service', label: 'Service' },
          { key: 'benchmark', label: 'Indicative benchmark' },
          { key: 'basis', label: 'Basis' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={rateRows}
        caption="Valencia FCL/LCL ranges are MEDIUM confidence from the Europe route data; rail, air, express and DDP dollar figures were not published and remain request-for-quote."
      />
      <SourceList sources={rateSources} title="Sources — Spain rates" />

      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Why the published lines stop at FCL/LCL:</strong> the research snapshot published Valencia
        20ft, 40ft and per-CBM ranges, but no rail, air, express or DDP dollar figure. Ask the forwarder
        to itemise freight, surcharges, THC, documentation, inspection, clearance, EU duty, 21% VAT and
        Iberian delivery on the same quote so the price is comparable across modes.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. Transit times by origin port and mode</h2>
    <p>
      The China-to-Spain clock is a planning range, not a promise, and 2026 made the Cape routing a new
      normal rather than a blip. The WorldFreightHub Europe route data puts sea transit at roughly
      <strong>32 days via the Cape of Good Hope</strong>, within a <strong>25–45 day range</strong>, with
      the Red Sea reroute adding about <strong>+10–14 days</strong> versus the pre-crisis Suez routing.
      China rail into Europe runs roughly <strong>18–22 days</strong>, but the final Iberia connection
      adds time — Valencia is not a primary rail terminus.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'origin', label: 'Origin' },
        { key: 'mode', label: 'Mode' },
        { key: 'transit', label: 'Indicative transit' },
        { key: 'basis', label: 'Basis' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={transitRows}
      caption="Indicative transit windows only — verify with the relevant carrier before relying on these figures."
    />
    <SourceList sources={transitSources} title="Sources — China to Spain transit times" />

    <div class="note mt-2">
      <strong>Sea planning default:</strong> Shanghai, Ningbo-Zhoushan and Shenzhen to Valencia are
      recorded at roughly 32 days on the current Cape routing, with the Red Sea reroute adding about
      +10–14 days over the old Suez routing. Direct calls are rare, so confirm the Med hub and feeder
      before committing to a date.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>4. FCL vs LCL: which fits Spain cargo?</h2>
      <p>
        The FCL-vs-LCL decision for Valencia starts with cube, urgency and handling tolerance. FCL suits
        cargo large enough to justify a 20GP, 40GP or 40HQ; LCL suits smaller consignments sharing a
        container. Using the published ranges illustratively, a midpoint 20ft rate of
        <strong>$3,150</strong> and an assumed LCL midpoint of <strong>$120/CBM</strong> break even at
        about <strong>26 CBM</strong> — a 15 CBM consignment would cost roughly <strong>$1,800 LCL</strong>
        versus a full container. That is a planning calculation, not a rate promise, so request quotes on
        both sides before deciding.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'factor', label: 'Factor' },
          { key: 'fcl', label: 'FCL (20GP / 40GP / 40HQ)' },
          { key: 'lcl', label: 'LCL (per CBM)' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={fclLclRows}
        caption="Decision support. Valencia FCL and LCL prices are MEDIUM confidence; the ~26 CBM illustrative split is a LOW-confidence planning calculation."
      />
      <SourceList sources={modeSources} title="Sources — FCL vs LCL decision" />

      <div class="grid grid--2 mt-2">
        <Card title="Choose FCL when...">
          <ul>
            <li>The cargo fills, or nearly fills, a 20GP, 40GP or 40HQ.</li>
            <li>You value a single sealed unit and lower handling exposure.</li>
            <li>The delivery plan can absorb the roughly 32-day sea window.</li>
            <li>The cargo is heavy, palletised or machine/project-oriented.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
        <Card title="Choose LCL when...">
          <ul>
            <li>The consignment is well under a container load — illustratively below ~26 CBM at the midpoint.</li>
            <li>You are running a trial order, mixed SKU retail fill or samples.</li>
            <li>You can accept CFS consolidation and deconsolidation time.</li>
            <li>Per-CBM economics beat paying for an empty container.</li>
          </ul>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>5. Ports: China origin ports and Valencia</h2>
    <p>
      The origin side is the familiar China port hierarchy: Shanghai and Ningbo-Zhoushan anchor the East
      China ocean services, while Shenzhen and Guangzhou cover South China and Qingdao, Tianjin and Xiamen
      provide northern/southeast alternatives. On the Spanish side, the destination dataset has one primary
      seaport — <strong>Valencia (ESVLC)</strong> — with Rotterdam (NLRTM) as the northern-range
      alternative when Iberia is not the only destination.
    </p>

    <h3>China origin ports</h3>
    <DataTable
